y my Delta X  that I'm using to approximate this inner  product ok and so once I add up those  first hundred terms in my Fourier series  I'm gonna plot my original function and  my Fourier series okay so a pretty  simple thumb code and this is what you  get so you see that there are kind of  qualitatively the right features you  know the white top hat function is what  I want to approximate the red curve here  is my hundred mode Fourier series and  you can see that it gets the right  general behavior but around these  corners there's this really interesting  ringing phenomenon known as a Gibbs  phenomenon so this is really really  important you'll see this all over the  place  this Gibbs phenomenon Gibbs is  phenomenon and essentially what it tells  you is that near points of discontinuity  your Fourier series if you ever truncate  it so if you added up all infinitely  many sines and cosines this would go  away in the limit as you know this one  hundred became infinity and I add up  infinitely many cosines and sines this  Gibbs phenomenon will in fact go away  but anytime I truncate my series for a  discontinuous function I'm gonna get  this this ringing Gibbs phenomenon so  I'm gonna get these kind of oscillating  artifacts at the points of discontinuity  okay and the reason is is because these  discontinuous points so the sine and  cosine functions are smooth they're  they're smooth functions they don't have  any discontinuities and so the only way  I can truly build this discontinuity  this this jump is by adding up sines and  cosines of all frequencies in just the  right proportion so that they stack up  to form this perfectly sharp Ridge and  if I drop even a few of those terms at  the end I'll get this this ringing  because they're not perfectly cancelling  out to form that steep that steep cliff  okay so that's Gibbs phenomenon you see  this all the time in numerix you'll  you'll you'll see it now that you know  it's there in in numeric sometimes  you'll see 