bsite and  you can kind of see this Gibbs  phenomenon getting worse and worse or at  least getting more localized to the  corners as you increase the order of  your approximation okay so the first  mode is just you know kind of this bump  in the middle and then as you add and  add modes they're kind of cancelling out  to make it flatter and flatter on the  top but then that concentrates the  ringing in these these corner regions  okay really important property  interestingly you'll see this I'm gonna  do this example later remember I can I  can for you transform this triangular  hat function but if I use the Fourier  transform to approximate its derivative  which looks like this top hat function  I'll get Gibbs phenomenon in the  derivative okay so I'll show you that  later when we use Fourier transforms to  approximate solutions of partial  differential equations okay thank you

